- 0.669x_{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186183116 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186183116 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "86574611-9912-59a0-a927-169448ee7cc9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186183117 #edb/ref 1080 "0.669(x_{1} + x_{2})" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186183117 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186183117 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ba60e21d-2efd-5690-8d25-a665e2d1a7c0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186183118 #edb/ref 1080 "0.669x_{1} + 0.743x_{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186183118 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186183118 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "bef64fba-d8d4-51d8-8dc1-f33c1cd7b71f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186183119 #edb/ref 1080 "0.743x_{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186183119 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186183119 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "04260e8a-d034-591f-90b2-b67f5f137aa8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186183120 #edb/ref 1080 "0.669x_{1} - 0.743x_{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186183120 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186183120 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "69f0d5be-23c6-52d6-9358-48cc078951e5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184045 #edb/ref 1069 "e-21882" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184045 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/31/31cc010ebe0a36d7c94ff0e5dd33c6c09a6d13c88f8c4696e32ce620dd037b00.png)\n\nThe scatter plot for a sample of paired observations is shown above. What vectors correspond to the direction of the first principal component of the data?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184045 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "db398e4a-388c-57d0-bc1b-9fb65d66707d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184045 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the *first principal component* of the data corresponds to the direction in which the data varies the most (the direction where we have the maximum variance).\n\nIn our diagram, the direction where the variance changes the most corresponds (approximately) to the vector $\\mathbf{a}$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/9d/9daed2ca60f9277d0b4b02c3c013446dc696960fc51065a83cd1c213a5afefde.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$\\mathbf{a}$ only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184046 #edb/ref 1069 "e-21885" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184046 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/9c/9c625bd3abc4fd02cf26119315911b24d06f8b31aa2977cf1571a8741ce091dd.png)\n\nThe scatter plot for a sample of paired observations is shown above. What vectors correspond to the direction of the second principal component of the data?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184046 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e71cf4f3-bd45-520b-86e6-22b348516139" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184046 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the *first principal component* of the data corresponds to the direction in which the data varies the most (the direction where we have the maximum variance).\n\nThe *second principal component* of the data corresponds to the direction perpendicular to the first principal component in which the data varies the most.\n\nIn our diagram, the direction where the variance changes the most corresponds (approximately) to the vector $\\mathbf{b}$, as shown below. So, $\\mathbf{b}$ defines the direction of the first principal component.\n\n![](images/2b/2b4539ab239ecf0e54d5e828079469b928dd9201ae5aa913db13104bf1ed1796.png)\n\nNext, notice that in the 2D case, we have only one direction perpendicular to the first principal component. And this direction corresponds to the vector $\\mathbf{c}$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/a3/a3d1e84cc1a20da39af058b422ea7860a11eb97c0afa59be752b0ca3ed0a14be.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$\\mathbf{c}$ only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184047 #edb/ref 1069 "e-21886" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184047 #edb/ref 1071 "A geologist has measured two specific features, $x_1$ and $x_2$, for each rock among a sample of rocks she is studying. The collected data was stored in a standardized observation matrix, where each row contains both features for a particular rock.\nThe first and second principal components of the data are approximately\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-0.447 \\\\ 0.894\\end{bmatrix}$\nBy combining the two measured features linearly, the geologist wants to construct a single feature that preserves the maximum amount of variance of the collected data. Which of the following could be the best candidate for the new feature?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184047 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "10f2c954-d70e-56eb-b981-ad393a620521" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184047 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall the direction in which the data varies the most (the direction where we have the maximum variance) corresponds to the *first principal component* of the data.\n\n![](images/97/97a46ed0c33579b24a5c43003b908ad7b04c78a3e5b5486d4bdc5d0fe2bb3bee.png)\n\nLet\n\n$$\n[ \\mid \\mid \\mid; v_{1} \\mid v_{2}; \\mid \\mid \\mid ]\n$$\n\nbe the matrix of principal components. Then, applying the change of variables $Y = XV$, we can convert our observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ of (linearly) transformed observations such that\n\n- the covariance matrix of $Y$ is diagonal (meaning that the new features $y_1$ and $y_2$ are uncorrelated), and\n- the features $y_1$ and $y_2$ are arranged in order of decreasing variance (meaning that most of the variance is concentrated in $y_1$).\n\nApplying our change of variables to a single observation $\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} & x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}y_{1} & y_{2}\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} & x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}V \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} & x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-0.447 & -0.894 \\\\ 0.894 & -0.447\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-0.447x_{1} + 0.894x_{2} & -0.894x_{1} - 0.447x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\ny_1 = -0.447 x_1 + 0.894 x_2\n$$\n\nwill contain most of the variance of the data.\n\nTherefore, the best candidate for the new single feature is\n\n$$\ny_1 = -0.447 x_1 + 0.894 x_2\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184344 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184344 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048834 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184344 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050727 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184344 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186184510 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184344 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186184512 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184344 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying the First Principal Component From a Scatter Plot" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184344 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186184045 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184344 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "240f83b9-6c11-53d6-a193-708221b59741" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184345 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186184511 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184345 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186184513 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184345 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying the Second Principal Component From a Scatter Plot" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184345 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186184046 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184345 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "f6314eaa-c493-5b5e-8488-3575762a00e0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184346 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050825 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184346 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186184751 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184346 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186184752 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184346 #edb/ref 1039 "Representing Principal Components as Linear Combinations" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184346 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186184047 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184346 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "de2ed4a0-7a12-5b55-adfc-32f705af4e99" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184372 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186185232 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184372 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186185233 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184372 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186185234 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [175